{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST tell agents that renaming or restructuring code in place, without changing behavior, needs no requirement review, but that the item's references move with it and the evidence that read it is reassessed.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST tell agents that renaming or restructuring code in place, without behavior change, needs no requirement review. It must also say that the item's references move with the code and that the evidence that read it is reassessed.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_needs_no_review_for_renaming_code_in_place]` is marked {test} for it. It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)`. That calls `assert_text` on `bundled_skill(name)`, which joins `SKILL.md` and its reference files and collapses whitespace. `assert_text` panics unless the text contains the phrase.\n\nThe asserted phrase is \"Restructuring code in place, including renaming an item, needs no requirement review, but references move with the code and the evidence is reassessed\". It covers every clause:\n- Restructuring and renaming in place.\n- Needing no requirement review.\n- References moving with the code.\n- The evidence being reassessed.\n\nDropping or reversing any clause makes the assertion fail. The phrase is a single sentence, so it also checks that the clauses appear together.\n\nI confirmed the phrase appears in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section. That is the skill the requirement names. The text is a static asset, so one phrase assertion is an adequate check. I found no gap and no missing marking.",
